  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Nicknamed the "Nice Colorado," the Gorges de Daluis wind between Guillaumes and Daluis in the upper Var valley, about 80 km north-west of Nice. Their red Permian pelite cliffs, up to 300 metres sheer, offer a spectacular mineral landscape, protected since 2012 as the first classified regional nature reserve in the Alpes-Maritimes, over more than 1,000 hectares. The reserve is managed by LPO PACA and the Alpes d'Azur community of communes.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Two ways to discover the site: by car, on the D2202 departmental road, whose footprint is excluded from the reserve's classified boundary, with the Pont de Berthéou car park (start of the Point sublime trail); or on foot, via the water hike along the gorge floor, which follows the Var riverbed and where the water has reached waist height according to the Visorando listing. This hike should only be attempted in good weather: the gorges are narrow in places with no escape route, and you should give it up if rain or storms are forecast. Inside the nature reserve (classified by resolution no. 12-1286 of 29/10/2012), domestic animals not on a leash are banned; the exceptions are animals used for farming, grazing and transhumance, those taking part in police, search or rescue missions, hunting hounds in the hunting season, and horses and donkeys on marked trails. Waste must be carried out. Wild camping is no longer allowed in the Alpes-Maritimes since the prefectural order of 20 June 2023, but the Roua bivouac area was still accessible according to LPO PACA (news item of August 2023).</p>
      <p>For a dog on foot without the water: the Point sublime trail starts from the Pont de Berthéou (marker 66), 4.4 km round trip, about 1h30, 237 m of elevation gain, rated "very easy" by Alpi Marittime Mercantour, up to a secured platform that overlooks the gorges by more than 100 m, facing the remains of the copper mines. That listing notes that dogs are allowed there on a leash and that bivouacking is banned. The reserve's manager points out that dogs, however well trained, are seen as predators by wildlife and can disturb it by leaving the path, and that an offence (dog off the leash, litter, camping between 9am and 7pm within an hour's walk of the road…) can be fined from €35 to €135. When approaching a flock guarded by protection dogs, the Natura 2000 newsletter of the community of communes advises keeping your distance, stopping, staying calm and keeping your dog on a leash. Motorised vehicles are banned in the reserve, but cycling remains allowed.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the Point sublime trail (4.4 km round trip, rated "very easy") is the simplest outing with a leashed dog; the water hike requires closed shoes and isn't suited to every dog (water sometimes waist-deep, several crossings of the Var riverbed).</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>According to LPO PACA, this red pelite landscape has no equivalent in France other than the neighbouring Gorges du Cians. The Guillaumes tourist office (04 93 05 57 76) assists visitors.</p>
